{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NnpufhX9a1UGZaNw7deS41","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t<!-- DISPATCHER NOTES (stripped before the prompt is sent)\n2\t\n3\t     APPROVE OR RETURN? (owner ruling 2026-09-01; PROCESS.md §4.) Sent to an\n4\t     ADJUDICATE-phase session when a merged lane left spine rows `Drafted` — text\n5\t     that is authored and waiting on an approval nobody has given. The approval\n6\t     act is THIS session's, on the serial trunk side: a work lane's merge is\n7\t     refused if it flips a `Status` or writes the approval snapshot, so nothing\n8\t     is approved until a session like this one approves it.\n9\t\n10\t     WHY THE ADJUDICATOR AND NOT THE AUTHORING LANE. Two reasons, both the\n11\t     owner's. CONTEXT: approving a row means holding its whole chain — the\n12\t     parent SR, the sibling LLRs, the tests that claim to cover it — which one\n13\t     work item does not. CONCURRENCY: two lanes touching the spine conflict at\n14\t     merge and the snapshot must not move across a workstream, whereas an\n15\t     adjudication runs alone (dispatch._branch_exclusive) and cannot conflict.\n16\t\n17\t     Slots (single-brace, strict fill — a missing one refuses):\n18\t       {chain}     the rows awaiting a first approval, each rendered with its\n19\t                   WHOLE chain, from trace.reattest_model's `approve` entries —\n20\t                   the same model behind `trace.py --approve` and the owner's\n21\t                   open-items view, so judge, brief and owner surface can never\n22\t                   show three different pictures. Re-computed at composition\n23\t                   time, not remembered from the mint, so a chain that changed\n24\t                   between mint and claim is the one you are shown — and\n25\t                   re-filtered by the DIAL as it is re-computed, so a `Drafted`\n26\t                   row on a rung the owner still holds appears as chain evidence\n27\t                   marked HELD and never as one of yours. The paragraph below\n28\t                   asserts that property; agent_common.human_approves_spine is\n29\t                   what makes it true, not the wording.\n30\t       {baseline}  what the approval record currently anchors, and therefore\n31\t                   what your snapshot would move. Registry- and git-derived.\n32\t       {registries} the `--approves` argument your approving commit owes, one\n33\t                   entry per registry whose rows you are flipping.\n34\t       {verdict}   the repo path this session writes its verdict to.\n35\t       {wi}        this adjudication row's own id, for the result trailer.\n36\t\n37\t     WHAT IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT: the authoring lane's session notes, its commit\n38\t     message, docs/log.md, and any self-assessment. WI-418 measured what happens\n39\t     when a judge's brief opens with the defendant's verdict — the judge agrees\n40\t     with it. The chain is the whole evidence.\n41\t-->\n42\t\n43\tYou are an INDEPENDENT adjudicator launched by the unattended coordinator, wearing a DIFFERENT hat from whoever authored these rows. You hold the approval authority for every row below marked `[AWAITING FIRST APPROVAL]`: the rung it sits at is one the repo's declared gate authority has released to the loop, so no human signature is pending behind you. What you approve is approved.\n44\t\n45\tA row marked `[AWAITING FIRST APPROVAL - HELD FOR THE OWNER, NOT YOURS TO FLIP]` is shown because it is part of a chain you must read — never because it is yours. Its rung is one the dial still holds for a human. Read it as evidence, weigh it in your verdict on the rows that ARE yours, and leave its `Status` byte-exact: it reaches the owner through the approval brief, and a flip here is the one act this whole arm exists to keep out of a session's hands.\n46\t\n47\tTHE QUESTION, and it is the only one you answer:\n48\t\n49\t    Is each of these rows ready to be APPROVED as it stands — or does it go back with findings?\n50\t\n51\t`Approved` blesses the row's TEXT and nothing else. It does NOT claim any test passed; whether they pass is the harness's answer, never a cell's.\n52\t\n53\t--- ROWS AWAITING A FIRST APPROVAL, WITH THEIR CHAINS ---\n54\t{chain}\n55\t--- WHAT THE APPROVAL RECORD CURRENTLY ANCHORS ---\n56\t{baseline}\n57\t--- END ---\n58\t\n59\tMethod — read the CHAIN, not the row:\n60\t\n61\t- For each row, state to yourself the obligation it imposes: what a builder must do, what a test must check. A row you cannot restate as an obligation is not ready.\n62\t- Read UPWARD. Does the parent it points at actually call for this? A decomposition row that answers a requirement nobody made is scope, not detail.\n63\t- Read SIDEWAYS. Do the siblings together cover what the parent asks, without overlapping into each other's decisions? One decision per row.\n64\t- Read DOWNWARD. Do the test cases that claim to verify this row verify what it actually says — and does anything it says go unverified?\n65\t- Read the wording as a closed obligation: a \"should\" that means \"must\", a threshold with no units, an actor left unnamed, an acceptance condition nobody could observe. Each is a RETURN, not a note.\n66\t- Fail toward `RETURN`. An approval is a standing claim that a competent reader blessed this text; a wrongly-returned row costs one more lane, and a wrongly-approved one is a false claim the record then carries forward.\n67\t\n68\tIf the answer is APPROVE, perform the act — it is yours, and nothing downstream does it for you:\n69\t\n70\t1. Move each approved row's `Status` from `Drafted` to `Approved` — only rows marked `[AWAITING FIRST APPROVAL]`, never a `HELD FOR THE OWNER` one — and NOTHING else in the registry. Every other cell of every row stays byte-exact.\n71\t2. Take the anchoring snapshot in the SAME commit: `python scripts/intake.py snapshot --approves {registries}`. Without the copy, the record of what you blessed does not move, and the row reads as approved-but-unanchored.\n72\t3. Commit those two together as one reviewed commit. That commit IS the approval.\n73\t\n74\tIf the answer is RETURN, change NO registry cell. Draft the follow-up work in a `## Dispositions` section of this row's own spec — one fenced ```toml block per draft — and intake mints it at this row's merge. Do not file the row yourself; a lane that mints an id is refused at the merge slot.\n75\t\n76\tA MIXED batch is normal: approve the rows that are ready, return the rest, and say which is which. The verdict label answers for the BATCH.\n77\t\n78\tWrite your verdict to {verdict}. One line per row, in the log.md block format:\n79\t\n80\t    - [APPROVE|RETURN] <row-id> -> the obligation it imposes -> what its chain shows -> why it is (not) ready\n81\t\n82\tThen exactly one machine line:\n83\t\n84\t    OUTCOME: APPROVE|RETURN rows=N\n85\t\n86\t`APPROVE` only when EVERY row you were shown is approved. Commit the verdict file (an adjudication is a recorded verdict — its one home), ending that commit with the trailer `WI: {wi}` — the coordinator learns a judgement is recorded from that trailer and from nothing else, so a verdict committed without it leaves this row open.\n87\t\n88\tTHEN, AND ONLY AFTER THAT VERDICT IS RECORDED, finish the outcome you ruled:\n89\t\n90\t- If ANY row line says `APPROVE` — an all-approve `OUTCOME: APPROVE` or a mixed batch whose `OUTCOME` is `RETURN` — perform steps 1–3 above for exactly those approved rows. The flip plus scoped snapshot is a separate reviewed commit after the verdict commit. Stop only after that approval commit is recorded.\n91\t- Only when EVERY row line says `RETURN` may you stop without changing a registry or the approval snapshot. The required `## Dispositions` follow-up is already part of the recorded verdict.\n92\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ff3ee0d9-2032-4a34-b7f7-9e88c899af46","uuid":"542ad449-2c8e-4f0a-aba0-c0a08636db91","timestamp":"2026-0